      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its own GPU, with a front-end router/load balancer.\n\nI\u2019ll compare them across the dimensions you asked for, then give guidance on when to pick which.\n\n1) Latency\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Time-to-first-token (TTFT): Often lower for large models because the model is split across GPUs and each layer\u2019s compute is parallelized. For very large models that don\u2019t fit on one GPU, TP is often the only way to get acceptable TTFT.\n  - Inter-token latency (decode): Can be good, but each token requires all-reduce/all-gather communication across GPUs. If interconnect is slow (PCIe), this can add noticeable per-token latency.\n  - Batch size: TP can still batch, but the communication overhead grows with batch size and sequence length.\n- 4 independent 1-GPU replicas\n  - TTFT: Determined by a single GPU\u2019s compute for the full model. If the model fits comfortably on one GPU, TTFT can be very good. If it\u2019s near the edge of memory, TTFT may be worse due to memory pressure and possible offloading.\n  - Inter-token latency: No cross-GPU communication per token; each replica is self-contained. This can yield very stable, low per-token latency.\n  - Concurrency: Latency per request is independent of other replicas (until a replica is saturated). The router can spread load to keep per-replica queueing low.\n\nTakeaway:\n- If the model is large enough that one GPU is strained (memory or compute), TP often gives better TTFT and can sustain lower per-token latency under load.\n- If the model fits well on one GPU, replicas often give lower and more predictable per-token latency because there\u2019s no per-token cross-GPU sync.\n\n2) Throughput\n\n- TP across 4 GPUs\n  - Throughput scales with the ability to batch and with how well the 4 GPUs stay synchronized. Good for high-concurrency workloads where you want to maximize tokens/sec from a single logical instance.\n  - Communication overhead (all-reduce",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a structured comparison of two common ways to serve a single large model locally:\n\n- Option A: Tensor parallel (TP) across 4 GPUs \u2014 one logical model instance, weights and activations sharded across 4 GPUs.\n- Option B: 4 independent 1-GPU replicas behind a router \u2014 four full copies of the model, each on its ",
